#
# Security region: surface, hardened
#
# What the kernel offers a process that is not meant to have it. The base
# configuration is already narrow (no bpf(2), no userfaultfd, no /dev/mem, no
# kexec, no debugfs, no magic sysrq: debug_none and the allnoconfig it starts
# from see to that); this region closes the ways in that a general-purpose
# machine keeps open for software this image may never run, and reports the
# ones a tier cannot take without breaking something the image needs.
#
#   sec_surface_hardened  a process cannot replace an ACPI table or an EFI
#                         SSDT from userspace, cannot ask for the CPU's
#                         model-specific registers or the raw cpuid through a
#                         device, cannot reach the I/O port space (ioperm and
#                         iopl), and sees no legacy vsyscall page
#   sec_surface_locked    everything above, and no 32-bit or x32 syscall
#                         ABI, no local descriptor table, no filesystems in
#                         userspace (FUSE), no binary format registration,
#                         no key and device notification queue and no POSIX
#                         message queues, and no io_uring or file handle
#                         system calls. A kiosk's program is a native
#                         64-bit one
#
# Not here, because the image stands on it and the build refuses to lose it:
# user namespaces (a bowl's program is root mapped into one) and seccomp
# filters (a browser's sandbox). A kiosk that wants user namespaces off sets
# user.max_user_namespaces to 0 from its own script.
#
#> includes sec_baseline
